00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.110
There's two main use
cases that we address--

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.750
speech for human-computer
interaction and speech

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.496
analytics.

00:01:49.496 --> 00:01:51.120
Human-computer
interaction is typically

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.090
one person talking
to a computer,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.590
and then a computer
reacting to it--

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:00.370
either voice search, or voice
actions, or things like that.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.240
Speech analytics is
analyzing conversations

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.380
between different humans.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.524
It could be in a phone call,
it could be in a meeting.

00:02:07.524 --> 00:02:09.690
So we're going to sort of
structure the conversation

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.310
today around those
two sets of use cases.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:18.390
So today, we're introducing four
new beta features, or actually

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.000
yesterday--

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.242
language ID,
multi-channel recognition,

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:25.200
speaker diarization, and
word-level confidence.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.033
We're going to talk
more about each of them,

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:29.940
so we'll come back to them.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.570
But they all make speech-to-text
much, much more useful

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.220
and practical for the various
use cases that it solves.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.630
So let's focus on the
first set of use cases

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.290
first, so human-computer
interaction,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.660
when you want to use speech
recognition to power sort

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.150
of different apps or solutions.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:53.040
So there, our new feature
we're introducing for that

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.470
is language ID.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.710
So like you can see on the
right on that screenshot,

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:02.970
for Google search, if
you use voice search,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.890
you can select multiple
different languages.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.015
So it's not a radio button.

00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:07.410
It's sort of check
boxes, and you

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.770
can check as many as you want.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.170
And then when you
speak to it, it

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.990
can understand which
language you're talking.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:19.150
And it recognizes the
transcription in that language.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.790
So we're now making that
available in the API.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.670
You just send multiple
language codes,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.830
we figure out which
one is the right one,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.450
and we return the
transcript in that language.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.870
It works pretty well, but
it's still not perfect.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.650
So if you actually know the
language code in advance,

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.490
you're always better
off just supplying it.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.640
But if you don't know
and you have multiple,

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.670
it's going to do kind
of a best-efforts job

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.790
at figuring out which
language it was.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.250
It's currently optimized
for voice search and voice

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.859
commands, so we
recommend using it

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:50.400
for those kinds of
use cases for now.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.370
And then for speech
analytics, the second set

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.370
of use cases we have, that's
where the other three features

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.190
we're introducing
today are focused.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.020
So let's talk a little
bit more about those.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.830
So speech recognition,
for people

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.450
that are new to the space,
it sounds pretty similar.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.300
You're trying to recognize
what someone said.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.420
It sounds like a
consistent set of problems

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.210
that you're solving, but it can
actually be quite different.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:28.920
So if you look at recognizing
a video versus recognizing

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.150
a phone call versus
recognizing a command--

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.640
an example of a video
could be a basketball game.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.910
And you could have sounds of
the different players that

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.490
are playing, sounds of
the actual basketball

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:47.280
bouncing on the ground,
sounds of the TV hosts

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:53.820
that are sort of interrupting,
sounds of advertisements that

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:54.750
are playing.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.280
And the number of speakers
could be four or more.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.160
It could be even like
20, or 30, or 40,

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.340
depending on how many
advertisements are there.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.310
There could be lots
of background noises,

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.950
and it could be many hours long.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.680
If you look at phone
calls, it's usually

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.980
going to be a
handful of speakers,

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.080
and the background
there is going

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.040
to be more static from the
phone line, or compression

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.470
that's used, or a narrower
sampling rate like 8 kilohertz.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:28.600
And it's usually going
to be a few minutes long.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.210
And then if you
look at a command,

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.210
it's usually just one
speaker, a few seconds,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.250
and there could be a
lot of background noise,

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.110
but it's very, very
different in nature.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.330
It's going to be other
people talking, or music,

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.565
or other things playing
in the background.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.150
A lot of Google's speech
recognition efforts

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.370
were originally focused
on the right-hand side,

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.490
on sort of voice searches
and voice commands.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:56.980
But one of the kind
of top requests

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.860
we got from Cloud customers is
to solve more of the problems

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.290
on the left.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:09.860
And so that's where, in April,
we introduced our new models.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.770
So we now have four
models that you

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.360
can choose from-- phone
call, video, voice commands,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:22.150
and default. And they each
are tuned to perform really

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.850
well for that set of use cases.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:31.150
So voice commands is tuned
to actually filter out

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.980
all of the other
background speakers,

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:37.870
because it's built to
recognize one person.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.970
And it assumes that
everything else that's

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.640
not the primary speaker
should be filtered out,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.700
versus our phone call
model is actually

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.370
built to recognize
everyone that's

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.770
talking in the phone call
that sounds like they're

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:52.900
part of the conversation.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.660
So they're kind of built
with different goals.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:03.072
Now, our default model that's
been around for a year or two,

00:07:03.072 --> 00:07:04.780
we've gotten consistent
customer feedback

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.034
that it's best in
the market, it's

00:07:07.034 --> 00:07:08.950
better than any other
vendor that's out there.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.740
But that was never
our aspiration.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.240
We want it to be not just better
than others, but actually good.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.650
And so that's where we came
out with these new models.

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.750
What we were able to do--

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:27.230
the video model is 64% less
errors than our default model

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:30.940
that was considered already
best in class in the industry.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:35.680
And so you could argue that it's
three times as good as anything

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:36.790
else that's out there.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:38.930
And then, the new
phone call model

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.960
is 54% less errors than
our previous model,

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.890
so twice as good as
what existed before.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:51.550
So we're very excited about
these new models and all

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.140
of the new use cases
that they open,

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:54.520
because now that
you can actually

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.490
do speech recognition for these
mediums that's pretty good,

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.947
there's all of this
set of analytics

00:07:59.947 --> 00:08:01.780
that you can do that
you couldn't do before.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:08.880
So today, we're introducing two
new features in this space--

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.330
speaker that opposition and
multi-channel recognition.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.250
So when you have a conversation
between multiple people,

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:17.290
our recommendation is to
record them separately

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.060
in separate channels.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:20.570
And there's two
reasons for that.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:22.482
One is the accuracy
of the transcription

00:08:22.482 --> 00:08:24.940
is going to be better, because
even for humans, it's easier

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.830
to understand each
speaker separately

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.660
versus when they're talking
on top of each other.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.180
The other reason
is it's also easier

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.729
to separate the speakers,
because you don't really need

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:35.020
to do anything algorithmically.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:39.169
You can just figure out like
who spoke in each channel.

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:43.870
So for that, if you do have
the speakers separated,

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:46.930
that's where we were introducing
multichannel recognition that

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.900
can take an audio file
that has multiple channels

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:51.700
and separate the
different channels

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:55.330
and the speakers there.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.770
Unfortunately, not
everyone has that luxury

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.900
of having the audio file
separated into channels.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.527
And so if you only
have a mono channel

00:09:04.527 --> 00:09:06.610
and you still want to
recognize multiple speakers,

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.630
that's where we're now offering,
in beta, our new speaker

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:11.740
diarization.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:15.640
It can separate multiple
speakers in an audio file,

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:17.830
but it's obviously not
going to be perfect.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:21.760
It's going to do kind
of a best-efforts job,

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.430
because it's using machine
learning and doing it

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:29.920
sort of via program versus
the external separation.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.220
Either way, we don't recognize
the identity of the person,

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.770
and we don't store
the information

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:38.660
beyond the API call.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.815
So there's nothing here
that's related to identity.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:47.440
So I'm going to show
you guys just an example

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.440
architecture of how this could
be used in speech analytics.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.000
And then we'll do a
little bit of a code lab.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.560
And you'll also see
another example of it

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.900
later from one of
our guest speakers.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:04.210
So you could imagine a setup
where you have audio coming in

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.170
from a phone gateway.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:08.960
A lot of these phone gateways
were built many years ago

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:12.500
and don't really have the
ability to separate audio

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.680
into different channels,
so it comes out as mono.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:18.080
And then, it would go
to Cloud Speech-to-Text

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:21.440
that does the transcription
and the diarization.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.380
And then if you want to do
cool things, like for example

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:26.150
analyzing sentiment,
you really want

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.370
to analyze each
speaker separately,

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.520
because what matters is
not sort of the average

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:36.920
of the both, but each
one as they were talking.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.410
So you can analyze the
customer and the agent

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:41.870
separately, and
then feed each one

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.240
into some analytics
engine like BigQuery,

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.450
and then run different
analytics on that data.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.510
So last but not least, one
other feature we're introducing

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:57.980
is word-level confidence.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:00.590
It's been kind of one
of our top features

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:05.210
from our ISV customers, more
sophisticated customers.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:07.250
What it does, it gives
you a confidence go.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.570
Up until now, like you
can see on the top,

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:14.180
we had confidence scores
that were based on segments.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.660
Now, you actually get confidence
scores based on the words,

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.230
so you can take
more action on it,

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:21.170
like we prompt in the
user or things like that.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:27.470
So I'm going to
show you guys how

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:33.800
to actually do this in Python.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:36.320
Bear in mind, I'm not a
very good Python programmer.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.870
So let's do this together.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:40.987
Hopefully it will work.

00:11:40.987 --> 00:11:42.320
If not, we'll solve it together.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:50.120
Actually, before we do that,
let me just show you the sample

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:51.456
file that we're going to use.

00:11:51.456 --> 00:11:52.580
So this is the sample file.

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:53.205
Let me play it.

00:11:53.205 --> 00:11:55.400
I hope the audio is connected.

