00:23:59.578 --> 00:24:02.210
SPEAKER 4: --only in English,
and also diarization--

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:04.080
NIKOLAY AVRIONOV: So
we were discussing

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:07.380
this exact presentation.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:10.440
So what was the process
for us to select

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.360
a speech-to-text provider.

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:14.520
We were not a Google
client at that time.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:17.940
We looked at all the major
speech-to-text providers.

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:21.270
We built our own use cases
specifically for what we do,

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:24.510
because we wanted to have the
best provider for our use case.

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:27.930
And what we selected to
the Google Video API.

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:29.760
We actually started
to work with them

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:31.740
when the API was still in beta.

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:34.860
And we released our
feature one month

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:40.470
after the Google Video
API went into production.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:44.060
So the takeaway
from here is really

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:47.480
test and find the best
provider for yourself.

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:49.920
You see these numbers
that providers usually

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:55.040
have accuracy, but it's
important what works for you.

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:59.010
How it's implemented--
before that,

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:00.400
we are mostly Amazon shop.

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:03.360
So we have our
real-time infrastructure

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.400
on Amazon or on premise.

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:08.460
And we have to connect our own
infrastructure with Google.

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:12.420
So currently, we have a
VPC between us and Google.

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:15.210
And we combine
several data sources.

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:17.730
We take all the events that
happen in the meeting--

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:21.930
when somebody speaks,
when he stops and starts,

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.900
the video streams, we
send the audio for Google

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:25.770
to transcription.

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.620
And the combined result is
what you saw on the screen.

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:32.760
We can identify every
speaker to a word,

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:37.090
and we can click on that
word and play from there.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:38.970
I'm showing you a few
code samples, probably

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:40.560
nothing surprising for you.

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:44.150
One thing that we
decided is to build

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:46.340
a set of services--
transcription,

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.600
parsing of the
transcripts as a service.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:51.790
So we can change
the transcription,

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:54.240
even change the
provider if we have to,

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:56.880
support multiple
transcription results.

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.400
So we have this layer
between us and the providers

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:04.170
that we can switch them
at any time if we have to.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:06.570
So the code samples
are pretty easy.

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:09.000
One thing that I
want to emphasize

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.750
is that it's really important
to handle all the error cases,

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:16.620
because sometimes for
something like speech-to-text,

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:19.260
you can see way more
errors than something

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:22.140
like a regular product.

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:25.480
You have a recording that
nobody speaks or doesn't

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:27.040
start and stop really quickly.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.520
So initially, you will
see a lot of problems

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:33.039
that are not problems with
the Speech-to-Text API,

00:26:33.039 --> 00:26:34.830
but just how the
customers use the product.

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:40.755
We can see that the results
were really positive.

00:26:40.755 --> 00:26:44.870
We are getting a lot on
Twitter, on other channels,

00:26:44.870 --> 00:26:48.370
we are getting a lot of positive
feedback from customers.

00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:51.180
We saw that the
usage of the product

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:53.190
is actually going
up because customers

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:56.614
are relying on the meeting to
be recorded and transcribed.

00:26:56.614 --> 00:26:58.530
So sometimes they tell
us that they don't even

00:26:58.530 --> 00:26:59.940
need to take notes anymore.

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:03.060
They know that this
is recorded for them,

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:05.290
and they just go and
review what people said.

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:09.720
What we're working
in the future--

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:13.440
we'll continue to work with Don
and his team, better accuracy

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:14.070
for accents.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:17.490
When we started to test
it, it works very well

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:22.000
for native speakers.

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:25.920
But it really has a problem with
English with different accents,

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:31.950
even accents like Irish are not
transcribed as well as English.

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:35.400
So we are looking
forward for that.

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:37.240
Multi-language support,
that's obvious.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:41.190
We support 16 languages, we want
to introduce all the languages

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:42.060
to the product.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:45.610
And speaker diarization, this
is very important for us,

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:48.480
because a lot of meetings
happen from a conference room,

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:51.709
and we cannot separate
the speakers using just

00:27:51.709 --> 00:27:52.500
our infrastructure.

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:53.800
We have to use the diarization.

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:56.850
We started to experiment,
we've been using the beta.

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:59.070
And again, we are applying
the same approach.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:01.920
We are going to build
our own test cases,

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.170
test it, and understand
what works better for us.

00:28:07.068 --> 00:28:08.034
Thank you, guys.

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:17.931
BOB MCKINNEY: How you doing?

00:28:17.931 --> 00:28:18.930
My name is Bob McKinney.

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:21.261
I'm a speech analytics expert.

00:28:21.261 --> 00:28:23.760
And I'm going to talk to you
about a really exciting project

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.980
that I've worked on
for the last year.

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:28.170
It's around retail.

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:30.560
Basically, if you've
ever worked in retail,

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:31.980
you understand
that retail stores

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:35.400
get a lot of phone calls,
from price and availability

00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:38.800
to just checking warranty,
things like that.

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:42.150
So one of the projects that I've
worked on over the last year,

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:47.070
I worked for two national brands
working on a yearlong project.

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:50.280
We analyzed over
360,000 retail store

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:54.420
calls, which translates to about
a million minutes processed.

00:28:54.420 --> 00:28:59.100
The goal of the project was to
focus on phone call conversion.

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:03.510
And this is a big deal, because
if you use AdWords or Bing

00:29:03.510 --> 00:29:07.350
ads, or any ads out
there that have location

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:09.210
extensions on them,
or click to call,

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:13.080
or Google My Business
ads, you really

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:16.710
want to be able to track the
performance of those ads.

00:29:16.710 --> 00:29:19.500
And that was the
focus of the project

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:22.260
to say, hey, what is the
conversion of those calls?

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:26.830
So using Speech-to-Text
APIs from Google,

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:29.190
speaker diarization
models, enhanced models,

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:31.630
the phone model is
the model I ended up

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:35.430
picking, natural language
processing for sentiment

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:37.530
analysis and entity analysis.

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.050
And then ultimately,
to connect it all back

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:44.220
to the internal system
of the two brands using

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:47.010
the internal
messaging bus, Google

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:49.010
has a pretty good version
of this, it's Pub/Sub.

00:29:49.010 --> 00:29:53.970
But in this case, we actually
used Microsoft BizTalk.

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:57.150
Just a little bit about
the architecture here--

00:29:57.150 --> 00:30:01.530
the company the partner decided
to go with for encapsulation

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:04.260
of the calls DialogTech,
a company out

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:06.300
of Chicago, great company.

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:11.640
Basically what happened is, a
customer would call the store,

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:16.200
and DialogTech
would do a post call

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:19.610
to a Cloud Function that would
trigger off a number of events,

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:22.530
one of those events
being download the wav

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:27.090
file from DialogTech into
the Google Cloud Storage,

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:30.120
which allowed you to do all
the things that Dan showed you

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:32.160
in the demo.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:34.500
You can call that
Cloud Storage bucket

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:37.470
from speech-to-text,
natural language processing,

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:39.490
and a bunch of other places.

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:41.754
So we're working
through that demo.

00:30:41.754 --> 00:30:43.170
Some of the things
we were looking

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:46.040
for on the output
of that in BigQuery

00:30:46.040 --> 00:30:51.000
were fuzzy string matching,
engrams, just the frequency

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.700
of words, so demand over time.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:55.590
So if a particular
product, you can

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:58.120
watch the demand over a
year period to say, hey,

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:00.360
is it ramping during this
season or that season,

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:02.670
or is it declining?

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:05.550
And that's a really big deal.

00:31:05.550 --> 00:31:09.119
Using BigQuery, and Python, and
some of the packages in Python

00:31:09.119 --> 00:31:10.035
allowed us to do that.

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:15.180
The biggest thing,
though, that helped out

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:18.480
the most was definitely
speaker diarization, which

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:24.660
allowed us to separate out the
customer from the associate

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:25.521
at the store.

00:31:25.521 --> 00:31:27.270
And this allowed us
to do a lot of things,

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:31.440
from A/B testing on the
associate side for training,

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:37.590
and then engrams,
and the fuzzy text

00:31:37.590 --> 00:31:39.940
matching on the customer side.

00:31:39.940 --> 00:31:43.149
So we could say, hey, if
they, in this particular case,

00:31:43.149 --> 00:31:44.940
in this call, we're
looking for phone calls

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:48.270
where they mention the
iPhone 7 and whether or not

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:51.180
it was a broken screen or
needed a battery replacement.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:53.820
In this case, we wanted to
say, hey, the broken screen,

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.580
we add that to our list
for frequency for engrams.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:00.844
And then, we can also go down
through that using caller ID

00:32:00.844 --> 00:32:02.760
and match it against the
internal system using

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:05.340
that message bus to say, hey,
did that phone call actually

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:08.730
convert into a sale?

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:11.250
And then, if you're getting
thousands and thousands

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:14.850
of calls a day, you
can actually figure out

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:16.890
the value of those
phone calls based

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:19.930
on that conversion
rate, which is, I think,

00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:20.860
pretty revolutionary.

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:25.740
So I encourage anybody that
has that kind of problem just

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:28.620
to keep working
through these APIs,

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:30.610
and you'll find a solution.

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:33.000
Some of the other things
that we could do around that

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.140
is the entity and
sentiment analysis.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:37.890
So in this case, you can
see that the consumer good

00:32:37.890 --> 00:32:40.920
being an iPhone 7, and you
could see the sentiment score is

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:45.150
a negative 0.4, which is
negative because they broke

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:47.350
the screen, they're
probably unhappy about that,

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:49.920
and they're just trying
to get it repaired.

00:32:49.920 --> 00:32:53.400
And you could see that in the
entity and sentiment analysis.

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.381
Why we chose Google
Cloud to Speech?

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:04.630
The accuracy of
the transcription.

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:08.670
With phone models,
we benchmarked

00:33:08.670 --> 00:33:11.910
against some of the major
competitors out there--

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:19.970
Amazon, Watson, IBM, IBM-Watson,
and the Microsoft products.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:25.370
And we just found consistently
that the transcription accuracy

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:29.690
from the Google
Speech-to-Text, it's

00:33:29.690 --> 00:33:33.930
significantly better than
the other options out there.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.890
The diarization
reliability, just

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:41.090
being able to distinguish
between the users,

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:42.620
was a big deal for us--

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:47.450
so between the associate
and the customer.

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:50.600
That way, we could do
training for the associate,

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:53.210
and then we could do a
conversion on the customer.

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:56.660
Ease of use-- we had
a lot of services

00:33:56.660 --> 00:34:01.520
already built for using
Google Cloud Services, so it

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:04.370
was really easy to be able
to connect them all using

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:08.210
a virtual private network
between the Google Cloud

00:34:08.210 --> 00:34:10.460
Platform and the
internal network,

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:12.739
and connected to BigQuery
and Cloud Functions.

00:34:12.739 --> 00:34:16.370
So all that combined
made it a no-brainer

00:34:16.370 --> 00:34:21.230
to go with the Google Cloud
Speech-to-Text product.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:23.436
So I'm going to hand
it over to Mahesh,

00:34:23.436 --> 00:34:26.060
and he's going to talk a little
bit about what he's working on.

00:34:34.014 --> 00:34:35.139
MAHESH BALAJI: Thanks, Bob.

00:34:35.139 --> 00:34:36.139
I'm Mahesh Balaji.

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:38.270
I lead the cognitive
computing and data sciences

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:39.980
lab at Cognizant.

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:42.469
Cognizant is a leading
ID service provider,

00:34:42.469 --> 00:34:45.110
helps clients across three
lines of services, one of which

00:34:45.110 --> 00:34:47.000
is digital operations.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:49.283
Among other things that we
do in digital operations,

00:34:49.283 --> 00:34:51.199
we also maintain and
manage service [? text ?]

00:34:51.199 --> 00:34:53.628
for customers, which
brings us to the case I'm

00:34:53.628 --> 00:34:54.711
going to talk about today.

00:34:57.600 --> 00:35:00.480
Late in Q2 of last
year, I was actually

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:02.060
associated with this engagement.

00:35:02.060 --> 00:35:05.230
Client is a retail chain
based throughout the Australia

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:06.547
and New Zealand region.

00:35:06.547 --> 00:35:08.880
The typical challenges were--
there was pretty high call

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:11.040
volumes, there was
long wait times,

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:14.190
primarily it's a 24
by 7 by 365 set up

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:17.850
where the concurrent calls
were average around 20.

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:21.070
Day peaks would hit
high 25s, season peaks

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:23.880
would hit anywhere
from 45 to 50.

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:25.420
So we were creating,
on an average,

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:27.750
75,000 tickets, unique ones.

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:30.140
And if you add the repeat
calls and the overlaps,

00:35:30.140 --> 00:35:32.280
we were hitting 100K, easy.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:37.180
And we had around 1,500
scenarios, or cases,

00:35:37.180 --> 00:35:38.730
if you want to say,
that we covered

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:40.350
as part of this engagement.

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:43.350
And because of which, it took
a lot of time to onboard,

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:46.320
even though we bucketed common
scenarios and trained agents

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:47.136
accordingly.

00:35:47.136 --> 00:35:48.510
It did take a lot
of time for us,

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:50.700
and attrition was not
helping the situation.

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:54.300
So it was fully human-dependent,
so there was a very high cost

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:55.210
to it.

00:35:55.210 --> 00:35:59.430
And because of all this, our
CSAT score was pretty average.

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:02.760
So giving a view of the
process flow itself--

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:07.890
users across 3,000 stores call
in for issues like printer

00:36:07.890 --> 00:36:10.770
cartridge not working,
the freezer is broken,

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:12.510
please tell me what to do.

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:15.750
That gets to the IVR system,
IVR system forwards that

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:18.030
the agent, whoever is free.

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:21.360
Agent listens to it, validates
the user's request first,

00:36:21.360 --> 00:36:22.770
and then understands
the context,

00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:27.900
and provides the response,
and the user and the agent

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:30.140
kind of did most
of the work there.

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:32.610
So fast-forwarding what we did--

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:35.460
we still had the human,
but he was actually

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:37.320
handling only the exceptions.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:40.530
We replaced the whole
human handling, all of it,

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:42.850
with a bunch of boxes.

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:44.089
So now, the user calls in.

00:36:44.089 --> 00:36:46.380
From a user perspective,
there is absolutely no change.

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:50.580
He still calls in a toll-free
number that still hits the IVR.

00:36:50.580 --> 00:36:52.380
Instead of going to
the agent, it actually

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:55.530
goes to the integration
framework, which is the IFX,

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.150
abbreviated there.

00:36:57.150 --> 00:36:59.140
That does the orchestration
going forward.

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:03.020
So we send that text to
Google Services, get the voice

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:06.520
clip transcribed to text,
forward that to dialogue flow,

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:09.470
match the intent,
get the response,

00:37:09.470 --> 00:37:12.180
put it back into speech, and
give it back to the client.

00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:14.540
And this flow keeps
going back and forth

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:18.030
until the system feels that it
has to either escalate it out

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:21.570
or the issues are solved.

00:37:21.570 --> 00:37:25.380
So once we put this in
place, we immediately

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:27.330
saw significant benefits.

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:29.910
The average call time
dropped from four minutes

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:30.750
to one minute.

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:34.030
And because of that, the call
rate time came down by 20%

00:37:34.030 --> 00:37:34.939
straight.

00:37:34.939 --> 00:37:36.480
The human is still
there in the loop,

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:38.250
but he's actually
handling the exceptions.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:40.240
He's not doing the
bulk of the work.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:44.460
And because of that, the overall
operational cost came down.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:47.860
And the quality of service
was consistent and better,

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:50.610
the CSAT score, by
default, jumped up.

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:52.950
So if I was going at 2x,
there's a clock running here,

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:53.700
and I don't have time.

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:55.590
I still have to give
you this, some Q&amp;A time.

00:37:55.590 --> 00:37:59.400
So that's all Dan's
problem, not mine.

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:01.944
And what did we
learn from all this?

00:38:01.944 --> 00:38:03.360
I thought, this
is more important,

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:05.190
this is where I want
to probably spend time

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:07.784
than the previous context.

00:38:07.784 --> 00:38:09.450
So don't bother
preprocessing the audio.

00:38:09.450 --> 00:38:12.930
We went through the
exercise of doing that.

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:15.330
So as engineers, we
jump into the conclusion

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:17.470
that we have to apply
filters for noise reduction,

00:38:17.470 --> 00:38:19.120
we have to do that, do this.

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:20.451
You don't have to do that.

00:38:20.451 --> 00:38:22.450
So as a matter of fact,
it is counterproductive.

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:25.460
So if you apply a lot of digital
signal processing algorithms

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:29.510
upfront, the output
confidence score is less.

00:38:29.510 --> 00:38:30.700
That's what we figured out.

00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:33.960
So if you have a
proper audio clip,

00:38:33.960 --> 00:38:35.820
send it, because
the API already does

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:38.010
most of that work for you guys.

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:41.460
And of course, the second
one was the phrase hint.

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:44.580
So this helped us a lot in
improving the overall score.

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:47.640
Given the fact that it
can take up to 500 words,

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:50.940
and if it's domain-specific,
it did a phenomenal job

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:54.780
of making sure the text
gets transcribed to the most

00:38:54.780 --> 00:38:56.340
accuracy possible.

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:58.604
And the format
sampling and encoding,

00:38:58.604 --> 00:39:00.270
I would like to add
the model also here.

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:02.400
Now that there's three new
models being introduced,

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:03.960
it's very important.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.870
When we started off, again,
the engineering hat kind of

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:10.030
comes in, and we say MP4 is a
better format, it's compressed.

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:12.750
We kind of can move data
around quicker and all that.

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:14.620
But that's a lossy format.

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:17.970
You're better off
compromising on certain items,

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:20.520
and pick a lossless
format and lossless

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:24.600
encoding so that the output
quality, the final intention

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:27.330
of having the audio clip
transformed into text,

00:39:27.330 --> 00:39:28.540
is better.

00:39:28.540 --> 00:39:30.210
So that's what I would
actually suggest.

00:39:30.210 --> 00:39:32.370
And I'm super excited
about the next one, which

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:34.780
is the word level
confidence, specifically

00:39:34.780 --> 00:39:37.140
the case I was talking about.

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:38.430
So it helps big time.

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:40.230
Most of the
conversations are short,

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:45.620
and you don't have to do a
full sentence or a phrase

00:39:45.620 --> 00:39:46.500
confidence.

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:48.530
You can go by the confidence
given by the word,

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:50.670
or you can pick and
choose the combination

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:54.510
to make sure the dialogue or
the conversation goes forward.

00:39:54.510 --> 00:39:58.590
Of course, all the new
things that got introduced

00:39:58.590 --> 00:40:04.210
does give a very, very natural
interaction between the user.

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:06.300
It is not IVR-ish anymore.

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:08.850
And that's primarily
a big difference

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:12.220
if you are counting the
CSAT scores and interacting.

00:40:12.220 --> 00:40:13.720
So if you're ever
on the other side,

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:15.553
and if you actually get
an IVR-ish response,

00:40:15.553 --> 00:40:17.220
I'm sure all of us hate that.

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:20.670
So that definitely has
come down with the new set

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:24.740
of models that's been
introduced by Google.

